{
  "id": "index",
  "title": "Big ideas start with a little code.",
  "eyebrow": "SMILE 2.0 / Learn to make games",
  "description": "Learn SMILE 2.0 through game-focused lessons, working code, diagrams, and a complete guide to syntax, built-in functions, libraries, arenas, and VFX.",
  "lead": "Learn to turn an idea into something you can play. A friendly guide to the SMILE 2.0 language, one small discovery at a time.",
  "body": "<div class=\"home-actions\"><a class=\"button\" href=\"start.html\">Write your first program <span aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span></a><a class=\"text-link\" href=\"reference.html\">Browse the reference ↗</a></div>\n<div class=\"mini-lab\" aria-label=\"Interactive coordinate lesson\">\n  <div class=\"lab-code\"><div class=\"panel-caption\"><span>Your first idea</span><span>SMILE 2.0</span></div><pre aria-label=\"Example drawing code\"><code><span class=\"token-keyword\">Dim</span> PlayerX <span class=\"token-keyword\">As Number</span>\n<span class=\"token-keyword\">Dim</span> PlayerY <span class=\"token-keyword\">As Number</span>\n\nPlayerX = <span id=\"lab-code-x\" class=\"token-number\">160</span>\nPlayerY = <span id=\"lab-code-y\" class=\"token-number\">100</span>\n\n<span class=\"token-comment\">' A position becomes a player.</span>\n<span class=\"token-keyword\">Fill Rectangle</span> PlayerX, PlayerY, <span class=\"token-number\">32</span>, <span class=\"token-number\">32</span>, CYAN</code></pre></div>\n  <div class=\"lab-preview\"><div class=\"panel-caption\"><span>See what changes</span><span>480 × 270</span></div>\n    <svg class=\"coordinate-canvas\" viewBox=\"0 0 480 270\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"lab-title lab-description\"><title id=\"lab-title\">A player on a coordinate grid</title><desc id=\"lab-description\">X increases rightward and Y increases downward. The sliders move the cyan player rectangle.</desc><defs><pattern id=\"grid\" width=\"30\" height=\"30\" patternUnits=\"userSpaceOnUse\"><path d=\"M 30 0 L 0 0 0 30\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#244254\" stroke-width=\"1\"/></pattern></defs><rect width=\"480\" height=\"270\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#102333\"/><rect width=\"480\" height=\"270\" fill=\"url(#grid)\"/><text x=\"12\" y=\"23\" fill=\"#c0d1df\" font-size=\"13\" font-family=\"Segoe UI, sans-serif\">(0, 0)</text><text x=\"361\" y=\"23\" fill=\"#93bad8\" font-size=\"13\" font-family=\"Segoe UI, sans-serif\">X →</text><text x=\"12\" y=\"249\" fill=\"#93bad8\" font-size=\"13\" font-family=\"Segoe UI, sans-serif\">Y ↓</text><rect id=\"lab-rect\" x=\"160\" y=\"100\" width=\"32\" height=\"32\" fill=\"#00ffff\"/></svg>\n    <div class=\"lab-controls\"><label for=\"lab-x\">PlayerX <output id=\"lab-x-value\">160</output><input id=\"lab-x\" type=\"range\" min=\"0\" max=\"448\" value=\"160\"></label><label for=\"lab-y\">PlayerY <output id=\"lab-y-value\">100</output><input id=\"lab-y\" type=\"range\" min=\"0\" max=\"238\" value=\"100\"></label></div>\n  </div>\n</div>\n<p class=\"lab-caption\">Try the sliders. Bigger X moves right. Bigger Y moves down. This browser illustration explains the drawing statement; you’ll run complete SMILE programs in the lessons.</p>\n<div class=\"feature-strip\"><span>No coding experience needed</span><span>Real SMILE examples</span><span>Learn by making games</span></div>\n<section aria-labelledby=\"learning-path\"><div class=\"path-heading\"><h2 id=\"learning-path\">Small steps. Playable ideas.</h2><p>Follow the path, or jump to what you need.</p></div>\n<div class=\"lesson-grid\">\n<a class=\"lesson-card\" href=\"start.html\"><span class=\"step\">01 / Get started</span><h3>Meet your new language.</h3><p>Set up SMILE, run a first program, and understand what each line does.</p><span class=\"card-link\">Make something run →</span></a>\n<a class=\"lesson-card\" href=\"basics.html\"><span class=\"step\">02 / Learn the building blocks</span><h3>Give your game a memory.</h3><p>Store health, names, and scores. Turn values into decisions and actions.</p><span class=\"card-link\">Learn values &amp; variables →</span></a>\n<a class=\"lesson-card\" href=\"game.html\"><span class=\"step\">03 / Build a real game</span><h3>Your first Star Collector.</h3><p>Move a player, collect stars, count points, and restart. Download the complete code.</p><span class=\"card-link\">Build the game →</span></a>\n<a class=\"lesson-card\" href=\"advanced.html\"><span class=\"step\">04 / Go further</span><h3>An arena. Three elements.</h3><p>Create a 3D stage, then bring Fire, Water, and Earth effects into the scene.</p><span class=\"card-link\">Explore arena &amp; VFX →</span></a>\n</div></section>\n<section aria-labelledby=\"game-thinking\"><h2 id=\"game-thinking\">A game is a story that repeats.</h2><p>Each frame asks the same three questions. What did the player do? What changed? What should they see?</p>\n<div class=\"concept-diagram\" aria-label=\"Input, update, draw, show screen, repeat\"><div class=\"diagram-step\"><strong>1. Input</strong>Read the controls</div><span class=\"diagram-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><div class=\"diagram-step\"><strong>2. Update</strong>Move. Check. Score.</div><span class=\"diagram-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">→</span><div class=\"diagram-step\"><strong>3. Draw</strong>Show the new frame</div><span class=\"diagram-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">↻</span></div>\n<p>You don’t have to understand everything at once. Change one value. Run it. Notice the difference. That’s programming.</p></section>\n<section aria-labelledby=\"made-with-smile\"><h2 id=\"made-with-smile\">Start small. See how far it goes.</h2><p>These are real projects from SMILE 2.0. The same ideas—values, loops, and reusable code—grow from a tiny game into a complete scene.</p>\n<div class=\"showcase-grid\">\n<figure><a href=\"game.html\"><img src=\"images/star-collector.jpg\" width=\"962\" height=\"572\" alt=\"Star Collector running natively, with a cyan player, yellow star, and score display\" loading=\"lazy\"></a><h3>Build this: Star Collector</h3><figcaption>Your first complete game. Shapes, keyboard input, collision, and a score.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure><a href=\"next.html#next-projects\"><img src=\"images/snake.png\" width=\"1000\" height=\"600\" alt=\"SMILE Snake teaching capture showing a snake on a grid and its score\" loading=\"lazy\"></a><h3>Next challenge: Snake</h3><figcaption>Practice arrays, grid coordinates, and the rules of a growing snake.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure><a href=\"advanced.html#fire\"><img src=\"images/fire-inferno-blast.jpg\" width=\"1440\" height=\"1025\" alt=\"The native Fire Lab showing Kael casting a bright jet of fire across a reflective arena\" loading=\"lazy\"></a><h3>Make an effect come alive</h3><figcaption>The advanced Fire Lab combines an actor, animation, and reusable fire emitters. The tutorial starts with a standalone effect.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure><a href=\"advanced.html#earth\"><img src=\"images/earth-boulder-hurl.jpg\" width=\"1440\" height=\"1025\" alt=\"Kael lifting rocks above the grid in the native Earth Lab\" loading=\"lazy\"></a><h3>Build a scene with structure</h3><figcaption>Earth effects, a shared arena, and a camera each have their own job.</figcaption></figure>\n</div></section>\n<section aria-labelledby=\"find-your-tool\"><h2 id=\"find-your-tool\">Looking for a particular tool?</h2><div class=\"lesson-grid\"><a class=\"lesson-card\" href=\"reference.html\"><span class=\"step\">Quick lookup</span><h3>The built-in reference</h3><p>Find a function’s spelling, parameters, and return value, with plain-language explanations.</p><span class=\"card-link\">Find a function →</span></a><a class=\"lesson-card\" href=\"libraries.html\"><span class=\"step\">Reusable building blocks</span><h3>The library guide</h3><p>Discover tools for movement, UI, RPG systems, 3D scenes, and effects.</p><span class=\"card-link\">Choose a library →</span></a></div></section>\n<div class=\"brand-note\"><img src=\"images/smile-2.0-logo.png\" width=\"768\" height=\"512\" alt=\"Official SMILE 2.0 logo\" loading=\"lazy\"><p><strong>Simple Modern and Intuitive Language for Everyone.</strong><br>Created by Louiery R. Sincioco. This guide teaches the current language through native Windows examples, from your first line to your own game.</p></div>",
